Adaptations in The Bugger
------------------------
The thirteen roles, in table order, are workspace, panel, raised surface,
selection, border, text, muted, attention, success, warning, key, type and
context. Role names in code retain the historical pink/green/orange/yellow/
purple/cyan spelling; they do not require those hues.

Gruber uses bg-1/bg/bg+1/bg+2/bg+3, fg, niagara, red, green, brown, yellow,
wisteria and quartz. Zenburn uses bg-1/bg/bg+05/bg+1/bg+3, fg, fg-05, red,
green, orange, yellow, magenta and cyan.

Modus uses bg-dim/bg-main/bg-inactive/bg-active/border, fg-main/fg-dim,
err/info/warning and yellow/magenta/cyan. Deuteranopia replaces the final
three roles with yellow-cooler/blue-warmer/cyan-faint; its err/info mappings
use yellow-warmer/blue. Tritanopia uses red-cooler/magenta/cyan-faint and
upstream red-warmer/cyan for err/info. This retains the variants' semantic
color substitutions instead of restoring the standard red/green roles.

Solarized uses canonical dark/light base surfaces and accents from
solarized-palettes.el. Dark panel/raised/selection use base02 over base03;
light panel uses base3 with base2 surfaces. Border and muted use base01/base1
in dark and base1/base01 in light; text uses base0/base00. Attention, success,
warning, key, type and context use red, green, orange, yellow, violet and cyan.

For all four families, foregrounds are blended toward text as necessary to
meet the existing 4.5:1 text and 3:1 accent/muted surface checks (text itself
moves toward black/white when needed). These are terminal role adaptations,
not unmodified upstream editor themes. Terminal quantization and UI pairings
do not inherit upstream accessibility certification. Non-color indicators and
8-color/monochrome fallbacks remain part of the interface.
